  "summary": "Preprints and one published study dissect cell-type-specific neural responses to developmental, immune, and aging perturbations in vivo.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is cell-type-resolved readout of neural tissue after a defined perturbation. In mice, neonatal SARS-CoV-2 spike protein exposure altered SOX2 cells and TBR1 neurons at P10 in a region-specific manner, without broad cerebrovascular or blood-brain barrier changes. Maternal TPOAb exposure in mice, reported in a published study, produced placental inflammation, fetal BBB disruption, hippocampal microglial activation, and altered BDNF and synaptic signaling. An enriched environment increased prefrontal perineuronal nets via NRG1/ErbB4 signaling and aggrecan in adult mice, with improved cognition. A multi-omic, spatially resolved atlas of spinal cord aging in mice and humans links senescence markers to specific cell types. Isogenic NGLY1-deficient human iPSC-derived models, which are not in vivo, show elevated RUNX1 and altered microglial development, phagocytosis, and pro-inflammatory cytokine expression. What is new is the resolution of these readouts at cell-type level, although most of the evidence remains at preprint stage.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
